A||N||7||Linx||"vatting"?||||5||
Z||000000||Linx||03-30-2000||07:57 PM||||I yanked my SE's v-6 last weekend, and delivered the block to a machine shop for boring.  They told me to come back in a couple days; I waited and returned to find they hadn't done it!<P>I'd asked them to clean the block.  The "vatting", as they called it, would cost me $14 extra.  No biggie.  But they never did it, because <I>I didnt include the crank bearing caps with the block.</I><P>Why do they need the bearing caps?<P>What they heck are they doing to my block?  <P>Does this process etch metal?<P>And rhetorically speaking...why the heck didnt they call me and say so!  I was hopin' to have it back in time to install this weekend.  Dang!<p>[This message has been edited by Linx (edited 03-30-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Rodrv6||03-30-2000||08:18 PM||||Linx,<BR>The main bearing caps must be installed and the bolts torqued in order to accuratly measure the cylinder bores and to do any type of machine work on the cylinders.  When the engine is assembled, the cylinder walls will change shape slightly due to the stress created by the main bearing cap bolts, so they must be installed when the cylinders are worked on.<BR>"Vatting" or "hot tanking" the block is a very common method of cleaning.  It does not do any damage to the cast iron block, but it does ruin the camshaft bearings and will strip all the paint off of the block.  The machine shop can install new cam bearings for a nominal charge, and you will have a nice clean block ready for assembly and painting.<BR>Rod||||reg||1
Z||000002||Linx||03-31-2000||12:35 AM||||Wreck my cam shaft bearings???  How?||||reg||4
Z||000003||N_Man||03-31-2000||12:45 AM||||What is your plan for the V6?<BR>stock rebuild? or performance?<BR>please let me know mine needs to be done.||||reg||1
