Z||000005||mrfiero||03-31-2000||01:00 AM||||The cam bearings are very soft and the corrosive nature of the hot tank will just destroy them.  If you are rebuilding the motor, then new cam bearings are a must anyways, so no big deal.  They must be pressed into place and is something that a machine shop needs to do...they have the proper equipment to do it and it's not that expensive.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Linx||03-31-2000||11:15 AM||||Just a stock rebuild.  The compression was so low and the oil consumption so high that it had to be done.<P>hey- does anyone know where I can find that metalized tape that's wrapped around the wiring harness where it enters the firewall?  Im re-wrapping all the wiring in the engine compartment, while it's away being cleaned.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||03-31-2000||10:33 PM||||Linx<P>The vat uses strongly reactive chemicals. Slightly acid as I remember. Quite literally the whole block get sunk in a tank full of cleaner.<P>Yes. The cleaning process will likely wreck the cam bearings. You need to replace them durring a rebuild anyway. If you are going to use the same cam you want to have it inspected and polished. You also want the crank inspected. If the crank needs work it may be cheaper to replace it with a reworked one. Depends how much work it needs.||||reg||1
